Runes — authors and contributors
================================

Runes started as clxcb-truetype, a port of clx-truetype to CLXCB.  This
file covers that part of Runes: the font manager, the rasterizer and the
X11 and Wayland bridges.  The OpenType loader (otf/) and the decompress
system (decompress/) have their own authors and licenses; see the
README and LICENSE in each.

Everyone below has worked on it or on the code it comes from.  Some of
their work has since been rewritten or removed in this particular port:
the CLX-specific code, the old manual and the StumpWM patch.  Even removed code was very
useful to me to gain the understanding of the library, so they are
credited for it all the same.

What clxcb-truetype added was the XCB port: the code that talks to the X
server through XCB (clxcb) instead of CLX.  Runes has since split the
code into a font manager and a rasterizer and added the Wayland bridge
and the layout functions (see Arthur Miller below).  The font class and
cache, metrics and rasterization at the core of it are clx-truetype's
work, carried over.  Credit for it, and copyright in it, stays with its
authors below.

The license, and what we could not confirm
------------------------------------------

clx-truetype was published under the MIT license (declared in its system
definition; it had no LICENSE file, and its source files carry no
copyright headers).  According to my online research -- public
discussions around clx-truetype and its forks, and the StumpWM port --
it grew out of the TrueType rendering written for McCLIM by Gilbert
Baumann and Andy Hefner.

I asked on McCLIM's issue tracker, on Codeberg.  McCLIM's maintainer
answered (October 2026) that McCLIM has always been under the LGPL,
version 2.1 or later, and that how and by whom clx-truetype was forked
is not known: either from McCLIM's code, or from code Gilbert Baumann
and Andy Hefner gave under a separate license.  That has not been
settled.

So this part of Runes is distributed under the LGPL, version 2.1 or later,
McCLIM's own license, which is right in either case.  The MIT notice
clx-truetype's code came with is kept in LICENSE, as the MIT license
requires.  If you are one of the authors and know more, please tell me
(see the issue tracker in the system definition).

The TrueType rendering for McCLIM, from which clx-truetype grew
(according to online research; see above)
---------------------------------------------------------------

  Gilbert Baumann
  Andy Hefner

clx-truetype
------------

  Michael Filonenko (filonenko-mikhail)
      Author and maintainer of clx-truetype, 2012-2016: the font class,
      the font cache, metrics and bounding boxes, rasterization with
      cl-vectors and cl-aa, the Xrender text drawing, bounding box and
      alpha map caching, the documentation and its metrics diagram, and
      the StumpWM integration patch.

  Kay Zheng (l04m33)
      Fixed resources leaked by DRAW-TEXT and friends (2016).

  madand
      Fixed a type error under (safety 3) (2020).

  Morgan Hager (goose121)
      Maintained the principal clx-truetype fork from 2021: font value
      caches with cacle, *ALLOW-FIXED-PITCH-P* (2025), and merging the
      other forks' fixes.

  Mihail Ivanchev (MIvanchev)
      Documented clx-truetype's situation and its principal fork (2024).

clxcb-truetype, now Runes
-------------------------

  Arthur Miller
      The port to XCB and clxcb (2026): drawables over XIDs with
      deferred, thread-safe frees, the Xrender path on clxcb's RENDER
      extension, locking around font loaders, and the fixes since.
      Then Runes (2026): the split into a backend-neutral font manager
      and a separate rasterizer (runes/raster), the layout additions
      (kerning offsets, line boxes, styled glyph runs), the Wayland
      bridge (runes/wayland) and the Antron rasterizer back end
      (runes/raster-antron).

This part of Runes is built on zpb-ttf, cl-vectors, cl-paths-ttf, cl-aa,
cacle, trivial-garbage and bordeaux-threads, and their authors' work.
